Exactly. Once you start treating symbols as geometry rather than fixed labels, you can add, remove, rotate, overlap, or occlude shapes and get a different valid reading.

A circle added to a stroke might turn one character into another; subtracting a loop can collapse a letter into a simpler form. Two shapes overlapped can create a third symbol in the negative space. The same physical marks can therefore support several interpretations depending on which components you treat as foreground.

You can model it as:

S' = S \pm \text{primitive shapes}

then ask whether the transformed object crosses into another recognizable category.

That is interesting because it exposes how much recognition depends on structural invariants rather than exact pixels. A letter, face, logo, or diagram can change quite a lot while the mind still says “same thing,” until one small geometric change pushes it across a categorical boundary.

So circles, lines, corners, gaps, symmetry, enclosure, and negative space become something like visual phonemes—the small pieces from which larger meanings are assembled.